{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's mechanism fails to specify the causal link by which a panel of non-experts, executing an expert-authored checklist, can 'correct a checklist item the original expert-author would have intuitively (and wrongly) marked as Pass.' If the checklist encodes the expert's process, the panel's application of it is causally downstream of the expert's knowledge. The target provides no causal mechanism for the panel to identify a *wrong* checklist item (i.e., an item that should have been marked Pass but is erroneously a Fail, or vice versa) without independent domain expertise. It asserts that 'internal panel disagreements and subsequent resolutions' correct the expert's error, but it never specifies what causal resource the non-experts draw on to determine that the checklist itself is wrong. Without this, the 'Error-Correction Trajectory' either collapses into mere compliance with a flawed checklist (which the target itself lists as a countercondition) or requires the non-experts to possess the very domain expertise they are stipulated to lack.","target":"9635645e6046ea008e7418d8ab1ca8954f663e2ce6f0c132c57fee2152df6408"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 fails the problem's demand to identify evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance. Its mechanism ('Cognitive Friction') explains *why* a panel might be slower, but 'forced deceleration of judgment' is causally indistinguishable from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. A panel of non-experts slowly and linearly ticking boxes is performing exactly the 'blind ticking' the problem warns against; the target never specifies the causal mechanism by which slowness *converts* into correctness rather than simply persisting as slow compliance. The mechanism provides no single-evaluation observable (as required) that separates a true gain from a slow, compliant coincidence.","target":"ed506fa140c67cf92e81da4c45106a27ad2fce09241663399815717cbc523693"}]}